# brew-to-macports — machine configuration
#
# Copy to one of these locations (first match wins):
#   $BREW2MP_CONFIG
#   ~/.config/brew-to-macports/tools.conf
#   <repo>/config/tools.conf
#
# This is sourced as bash. Environment variables win over this file, so assign
# with := only if you want the file to be authoritative.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- vendors ---
# Tools the script installs from the vendor's own binary release. Each name
# needs a latest_<tool> and install_<tool> pair in bin/upgrade-vendor-packages.sh
# plus an entry in the architecture map, so do not invent names here — add the
# functions first.
ALL_TOOLS="gh go node pandoc yt-dlp terraform ffmpeg deno resvg gws gcloud"

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ casks ---
# Homebrew casks that stay in brew but are upgraded on MAJOR changes only.
# They are held with `brew pin`, so plain `brew upgrade` never touches them;
# this script unpins, upgrades, and pins again. Use it for apps that ship a
# full-size installer on almost every patch release, where each patch means
# re-downloading the whole thing. Leave empty if you have none.
MAJOR_ONLY_CASKS=""

# -------------------------------------------------------------- locations ---
# VENDOR_ROOT="$HOME/.local/vendor"
# VENDOR_BIN_DIR="$HOME/.local/bin"

# How many old versions of each tool to keep for --rollback.
# KEEP_VERSIONS=2

# Interpreter for the embedded report code. Apple's system python is the
# default on purpose: it exists on every macOS, and the embedded snippets are
# deliberately kept 3.9-compatible so the script also runs on older releases.
# PY=/usr/bin/python3

# ------------------------------------------------------- MacPorts watchlist --
# Extra "port:brew-formula-it-replaces" pairs to watch for an Intel archive.
# The script rebuilds its own list from what is installed, so this is only for
# ports you want tracked before the matching formula exists locally.
# MACPORTS_WATCH="librsvg:librsvg
# imagemagick:imagemagick"
